{
  "dom_id": "step-q65411",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, then what is ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ In the given interval, the excluded value is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $  are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2.$ The solution to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{3}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\pi =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
